---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import katex from "katex";
import { board6 } from "../lib/board6";

const k = (tex: string) => katex.renderToString(tex, { throwOnError: true });
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.endsWith("/")
  ? import.meta.env.BASE_URL
  : `${import.meta.env.BASE_URL}/`;

const anatomy1 = board6({
  labels: [
    [2, 2, "c"],
    [1, 2, "a"],
    [3, 2, "a"],
    [2, 1, "a"],
    [2, 3, "a"],
    [1, 1, "d"],
    [1, 3, "d"],
    [3, 1, "d"],
    [3, 3, "d"],
  ],
  squares: [[1, 1, 1]],
});
const anatomy2 = board6({
  labels: [
    [3, 2, "c"],
    [1, 2, "a"],
    [5, 2, "a"],
    [3, 0, "a"],
    [3, 4, "a"],
    [1, 0, "d"],
    [1, 4, "d"],
    [5, 0, "d"],
    [5, 4, "d"],
  ],
  squares: [[1, 0, 2]],
});
const basic = board6({
  black: [
    [2, 2],
    [3, 2],
    [2, 1],
    [1, 2],
    [2, 3],
  ],
  white: [
    [0, 0],
    [4, 4],
    [3, 1],
    [1, 1],
  ],
  threats: [
    [1, 3],
    [3, 3],
  ],
});
const tempoEnd = board6({
  black: [
    [2, 2],
    [3, 2],
    [3, 3],
    [4, 3],
    [3, 4],
    [1, 2],
    [5, 2],
    [3, 0],
  ],
  white: [
    [0, 0],
    [3, 1],
    [2, 3],
    [4, 2],
    [4, 4],
    [1, 4],
    [5, 4],
  ],
  threats: [
    [1, 0],
    [5, 0],
  ],
});
---

<Base
  title="How the proof works · The Square Achievement Game"
  description="An illustrated guide to the proof that the first player wins the square achievement game exactly when n is at least 5: the plus rule, the 5 by 5 table, the draws, one run for every larger board, and the Lean formalization."
>
  <header class="masthead">
    <h1>How the proof works</h1>
    <p class="byline">An illustrated guide to the square achievement game</p>
  </header>

  <p class="abstract">
    <strong>Summary.</strong> Two players alternately claim cells of an
    <span set:html={k("n \\times n")} /> grid; the first to hold all four corners
    of an axis-aligned square wins. The first player has a winning strategy exactly
    when <span set:html={k("n \\ge 5")} />, and every smaller board is drawn
    under optimal play. From 6 × 6 up the winning strategy is a rule built on
    one shape, the plus, with at most three preparatory forcing moves; it is
    verified by exhaustive search on the boards from 6 × 6 to 17 × 17, a run on
    the 23 × 23 board is carried to every larger board, and the whole argument,
    the draws and the 5 × 5 board included, is formalized in Lean 4.
  </p>

  <figure class="game">
    <sag-board n="6"></sag-board>
    <figcaption>
      Figure 1. The bot follows the rule described below and explains each move.
      Change n to explore.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h2>The game</h2>
  <p>
    Played on the cells of an <span set:html={k("n \\times n")} /> grid, the first
    player moving first. A winning set is any
    <span
      set:html={k("\\{(r,c),\\,(r,c{+}s),\\,(r{+}s,c),\\,(r{+}s,c{+}s)\\}")}
    />
    with <span set:html={k("s \\ge 1")} />: the four corners of an axis-aligned
    square, from 2 × 2 blocks up. A full board with no completed square is a
    draw. Tilted squares do not count. Both players are after the same squares,
    so even a player who cannot win anymore can still block, and on the boards
    below 5 × 5 blocking is enough: neither player can force a win.
  </p>

  <div class="theorem">
    <span class="label">Theorem.</span> The first player has a winning strategy in
    the square achievement game on the <span set:html={k("n \\times n")} /> board
    if and only if <span set:html={k("n \\ge 5")} />. For
    <span set:html={k("n \\ge 6")} /> the first player following the plus rule wins
    by the seventeenth ply.
  </div>

  <h2>History</h2>
  <p>
    Harary called games like this achievement games: both players build toward
    the same target, and the first to finish wins <a class="cite" href="#ref-1"
      >[1]</a
    >; the convention goes back to the positional games of Hales and Jewett
    <a class="cite" href="#ref-8">[8]</a>. Erickson asked what happens for the
    axis-aligned square on an <span set:html={k("n \\times n")} /> grid
    <a class="cite" href="#ref-2">[2]</a>, and the question is recorded on the
    Open Problem Garden <a class="cite" href="#ref-3">[3]</a>. Two ends of the
    answer were known. Jenrich reported computer searches giving draws on 3 × 3
    and 4 × 4 and a first-player win on 5 × 5 <a class="cite" href="#ref-4"
      >[4]</a
    >. Bacher and Eliahou proved that every two-coloring of a 15 × 15 grid
    contains a monochromatic square <a class="cite" href="#ref-5">[5]</a>, so no
    game on a board of size 15 or more can end in a draw, and strategy stealing
    then gives the first player a win, though it produces no strategy. In
    between, from 6 × 6 through 14 × 14, nothing was published until August
    2026, when Keith Adler announced computer searches settling those nine sizes
    as first-player wins, with full strategy trees certified for 6 × 6 and 7 × 7
    and search verdicts for the rest, one search per size <a
      class="cite"
      href="#ref-9">[9]</a
    >. Independently and at the same time, this work took a different route: one
    rule for every board, machine-checked for all sizes at once. The bot in
    Figure 1 is playing it.
  </p>

  <h2>Pluses</h2>
  <p>
    A <em>plus</em> of side <span set:html={k("s")} /> is a centre cell with its four
    <em>arms</em>, the cells at distance <span set:html={k("s")} /> above, below,
    left, and right, and its four <em>diagonals</em>, the cells at distance <span
      set:html={k("s")}
    /> in both directions. The centre, two perpendicular arms, and the diagonal between
    them are the four corners of a square of side <span set:html={k("s")} />. So
    once we hold the centre and one arm, each further arm perpendicular to a
    held arm makes three corners of such a square, and the opponent must block
    at the diagonal or lose; and once we hold two opposite arms, the next arm is
    perpendicular to both and threatens two diagonals at once, which no single
    block answers. Inside the 6 × 6 corner of the board there are sixteen pluses
    of side 1 and four of side 2; side 3 does not fit.
  </p>

  <figure class="diagram">
    <svg
      viewBox="-4 -4 316 152"
      role="img"
      aria-label="Two 6 by 6 grids: the plus of side 1 at (2,2) and the plus of side 2 at (3,2), with centre, arms, and diagonals labelled"
    >
      <defs>
        <pattern
          id="grid6p"
          width="24"
          height="24"
          patternUnits="userSpaceOnUse"
        >
          <path
            d="M24 0H0V24"
            fill="none"
            stroke="var(--rule)"
            stroke-width="0.8"
            opacity="0.5"></path>
        </pattern>
      </defs>
      <g set:html={anatomy1} />
      <g transform="translate(164 0)" set:html={anatomy2} />
    </svg>
    <figcaption>
      Figure 2. Left, the plus of side 1 at (2,2); right, the plus of side 2 at
      (3,2). The centre is c, the arms a, the diagonals d. The dashed outline
      encloses one of the four squares of each plus.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>

  <p>
    The opening (2,2) and the second move (3,2) are the centre and an arm of the
    side-1 plus at (2,2), and an arm and the centre of the side-1 plus at (3,2).
    Against most replies one of these pluses wins outright, in three moves.
  </p>

  <figure class="diagram">
    <svg
      viewBox="-4 -4 152 152"
      role="img"
      aria-label="The end of the basic line: black holds the plus of side 1 at (2,2) except the last arm's threats"
    >
      <g set:html={basic} />
    </svg>
    <figcaption>
      Figure 3. The basic line against the replies (0,0) and (4,4). The arms
      (2,1) and (1,2) each forced a block on a diagonal; the last arm (2,3)
      threatens at both dashed cells; the win comes on the eleventh ply.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h2>Tempo moves and lines</h2>
  <p>
    When the opponent's stones spoil both pluses, sitting on an arm or a
    diagonal, the rule first plays <em>tempo moves</em>: single forcing moves,
    each threatening exactly one square, whose block leaves the opponent without
    a threat of their own. A tempo move and its block add one stone to each side
    and hand the move back to us, and our new stone is where we chose it. A
    <em>line</em> is a sequence of at most three tempo moves followed by the arms
    of one plus, each arm before the last forcing a block and the last making two
    threats. The rule prefers lines with fewer tempo moves, then fewer arms left to
    play, then the smaller plus, then the earliest cells, and it commits to the chosen
    line after the opponent's second reply. Every line has at most six moves, and
    the win comes by the seventeenth ply of the game at the latest.
  </p>

  <figure class="diagram">
    <svg
      viewBox="-4 -4 152 152"
      role="img"
      aria-label="The end of the longest line: two tempo moves prepared the plus of side 2 at (3,2)"
    >
      <g set:html={tempoEnd} />
    </svg>
    <figcaption>
      Figure 4. The longest line, against the replies (0,0) and (3,1). The tempo
      moves (3,3) and (4,3) were each blocked; then the arms (3,4), (1,2), (5,2)
      of the side-2 plus at (3,2) each forced a block, and the last arm (3,0)
      threatens at both dashed cells.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h2>The hidden corner</h2>
  <p>
    The rule reads only an abstraction of the position: our stones, which never
    leave the 6 × 6 corner, the opponent's stones inside the 17 × 17 base board,
    and one more cell. Two opponent stones far from the corner, each with a
    coordinate of 12 or more, can be two corners of a square whose third corner
    lies in the 6 × 6 region; we call that region cell their <em
      >hidden corner</em
    >. If the opponent were ever forced to block there, they would hold three
    corners of that square and threaten at its fourth, which may lie beyond
    anything the rule looks at. So the rule never makes a threat whose only
    block is the hidden corner. A containment lemma from the earlier version of
    this work says this is all the far stones can do: two stones of the corner
    region lie in a common square only if that square fits inside the 12 × 12
    board, so a far stone shares no square with two of the opponent's forced
    blocks, and a square through a far stone and one block has both free replies
    as its other corners.
  </p>

  <h2>What is checked by hand and what by machine</h2>
  <p>
    Why arms force, why the last arm doubles, and why the abstraction sees
    everything that matters are short arguments written out in the paper. That
    every reply pair has a line, and that following it wins against every reply,
    is a case analysis too large for a page and is done by a checker. The
    checker walks the <em>forced tree</em>: every reply at the first two levels,
    and below that only the replies that block a threat, since a reply that
    blocks nothing loses to the standing threat at once. At every node it
    evaluates the rule afresh; no table is consulted anywhere. The runs for the
    boards from 6 × 6 to 17 × 17 take from under a second to 75 seconds each.
  </p>
  <p>
    Rules are only as trustworthy as the programs that check them, so the rule
    exists three times, in Python, in Lean, and in TypeScript for the bot on
    this page. The Lean version is the one the proof cites, and its checker
    comes with a machine-checked soundness theorem. The three are compared on
    the same positions: the pipeline exports the line the rule commits to for
    every reply pair on the 6 × 6 board and for the anchored positions of the
    next section, and the TypeScript rule is replayed against that fixture in
    the site's tests.
  </p>

  <h2>Every board at once</h2>
  <p>
    Boards from 17 × 17 up are covered by the simulation argument of the earlier
    version of this work: a checked run on a base board is replayed on any
    larger board, with replies inside the base board read verbatim and replies
    beyond it replaced by stand-in cells. For the rule the base board is 23 ×
    23, the smallest that holds the stand-ins, and three families of anchored
    runs, 176 positions in all, cover the reply pairs with a hidden corner, with
    the hidden corner as an extra guard. Since the rule reads only the
    abstraction, and the stand-ins lie beyond the 17 × 17 board and share the
    hidden corner with the stones they replace, the rule computes the same move
    on the real board and on the base board; this agreement lemma is what makes
    the replayed run the rule's own play rather than merely a win found by the
    checker.
  </p>

  <h2>What the formal theorem says</h2>
  <p>
    The earlier version of this work proved <code>P1CanWin</code>: a winning
    strategy tree exists, namely one the checker found. This result proves more.
    The predicate
    <code>WinsWith σ n h</code> says that the game tree in which the first player
    plays the move of the strategy <code>σ</code> at every node after the history
    <code>h</code>, against every legal reply, ends in a first-player square on
    every branch. The theorem is
  </p>
  <pre
    class="lean"><code>theorem plus_wins_with {"{"}n : ℕ{"}"} (h : 6 ≤ n) :
    WinsWith plusMove n []</code></pre>
  <p>
    where <code>plusMove</code> is the rule as a Lean definition, 250 lines with no
    data beyond the seven exceptional second moves. Its axioms are the usual three
    of Lean plus the six evaluated certificates, the runs at 6 to 16, at 17, on the
    23 × 23 board, and the three anchored families, discharged by
    <code>native_decide</code>. Both artifacts are built from this repository on
    every deploy:
    <a href={`${base}downloads/paper.pdf`}>the paper (PDF)</a> and
    <a href={`${base}downloads/square-achievement-formalization.tar.gz`}
      >the Lean development (tar.gz)</a
    >.
  </p>

  <h2>How the rule was found</h2>
  <p>
    An earlier version of this work won the boards from 6 × 6 up with a lookup
    table of several thousand forcing plans, one per pair of opponent replies,
    found by search. Reading the plans rather than counting them, almost all
    began with an arm of the side-1 plus at (2,2) or (3,2), the shortest ones
    were that plus, and the long ones, replayed in the positions where they were
    used, either forced a block so as to place a stone a later square needed or
    played the arms of the side-2 plus at (3,2) one at a time. A first attempt
    at a rule scored moves by how many threats they made available and failed on
    a few dozen reply pairs near the opening; asking instead which plus can be
    completed by force, after a few tempo moves, gave a rule that wins them all.
  </p>

  <h2>The draws and the 5 × 5 board</h2>
  <p>
    Below 5 × 5 the first player cannot force a win: a dual search, also proved
    sound in Lean, exhibits a reply to every first-player move after which no
    win exists. That the boards are drawn, rather than lost for the first
    player, takes two more steps, both formalized. Strategy stealing shows the
    second player never forces a win on any board: a second-player strategy
    could be borrowed by the first player after an arbitrary opening move, since
    a surplus stone never hurts. Determinacy shows that a player who cannot be
    beaten can secure a draw. Together they give each player a drawing strategy
    on every board up to 4 × 4. The 5 × 5 board is won, but no purely forcing
    strategy from (2,2) exists there: from twenty reply pairs every all-forcing
    continuation fails. Its strategy is a table with quiet moves at branch
    points, extracted from a full solution of the 5 × 5 game and verified over
    the complete game tree; it plays on this page when you set n to 5.
  </p>

  <h2>Open questions</h2>
  <p>
    The rule wins by ply 17. What is the least ply by which the first player can
    force a win on the <span set:html={k("n \\times n")} /> board, and is there a
    rule of comparable length that attains it on every board? The second move has
    seven exceptions; is there an opening from which a rule of this kind needs none?
    The rule does not apply to 5 × 5, where no purely forcing strategy from (2,2)
    exists; a short rule for that board is open. And are the constants least: the
    6 × 6 region and the 17 × 17 base board?
  </p>

  <h2>References</h2>
  <ol class="refs">
    <li id="ref-1">
      F. Harary, Achievement and avoidance games for graphs, Annals of Discrete
      Mathematics 13 (1982), 111–119.
    </li>
    <li id="ref-2">
      M. Erickson, Pearls of Discrete Mathematics, CRC Press, 2010.
    </li>
    <li id="ref-3">
      M. Erickson, Square achievement game on an n × n grid, Open Problem
      Garden.
      <a href="http://www.openproblemgarden.org/op/a_game_on_an_n_x_n_grid"
        >openproblemgarden.org</a
      >
    </li>
    <li id="ref-4">
      T. Jenrich, Guaranteed successful strategies for a square achievement game
      on an n × n grid, 2012.
      <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/1109.2341">arXiv:1109.2341</a>
    </li>
    <li id="ref-5">
      R. Bacher and S. Eliahou, Extremal binary matrices without constant
      2-squares, Journal of Combinatorics 1 (2010), no. 1, 77–100.
    </li>
    <li id="ref-6">
      L. de Moura and S. Ullrich, The Lean 4 theorem prover and programming
      language, Automated Deduction – CADE 28, Springer, 2021.
    </li>
    <li id="ref-7">
      The mathlib Community, The Lean mathematical library, CPP 2020.
    </li>
    <li id="ref-8">
      A. W. Hales and R. I. Jewett, Regularity and positional games,
      Transactions of the American Mathematical Society 106 (1963), 222–229.
    </li>
